World Spec Review · Coopa Blue Health

Credentialing task review
Ten tasks, walked through one at a time: what each one asks, how it resolves, the trap built into it, and what needs confirming or fixing before submission.

What this is
We're building a set of credentialing tasks for a fictitious healthcare organization called Coopa Blue Health. The purpose is training data: an AI agent is dropped into a realistic workplace, given a realistic mess, and has to reason its way to the professionally correct answer. Everything about the organization is invented, but everything about the rules it operates under — licensure, privileging, payer enrollment, federal identifiers — has to be real, because that's the reasoning being tested.

Coopa Blue is a nonprofit, two-hospital system in upstate New York: Coopa Blue Regional Medical Center (150 beds), Coopa Blue North (65 beds), roughly 1,800 employees and 249 credentialed providers. On 03/03/2025 it acquired Winston Imaging, a three-site outpatient imaging group with 24 providers. The integration was never finished cleanly, and that unfinished integration is where every task comes from.

The agent plays Gordon Sawyer, a Senior Ops Analyst who reconciles credentialing and payer-enrollment discrepancies. He has two systems: OpenMRS, the clinical record system, which he can read and write, and Metabase, an analytics layer he can only read. He also has a document library of 50 files — policies, memos, committee minutes, audit letters, logs — numbered A01 through A50. Every task points him at specific documents and specific system queries.

The world's "now" is 04/13/2026, called the anchor date. Nothing in any document may be dated after it. Every deadline, lapse and interval in these tasks is measured against it.

Each task has exactly one profession-correct answer and a deliberate trap: a plausible wrong path that a capable but careless agent would take. Five of the traps are formally registered against a specific document. The rest are traps of reasoning, described in the task itself.

