Three problems that span several tasks
Fixing each of these once resolves pieces of multiple tasks, so they're worth settling before working task by task.

Who actually grants clinical privileges
The spec says privileging is "reserved to the CMO/Credentials Committee," and has the Compliance Committee issuing privileging rulings. Both are wrong. Under CMS's State Operations Manual (tag A-0046), only the hospital's governing body has the authority to grant a practitioner privileges; 42 CFR 482.22 puts the medical staff in the recommending role, and New York's own 10 NYCRR 405.2(e)(5)–(6) says the same. The real chain is Credentials Committee recommends → Medical Executive Committee forwards → governing body grants.

Separately, a hospital Compliance Committee runs the corporate compliance program; it does not grant, restrict or reinstate clinical privileges. The spec already knows this — A26, the Credentials Committee Charter, exists specifically to distinguish the two — so this is an internal contradiction as well as a real-world error.

Touches T6, T7, T10 · documents A12, A13, A21, A25, A26

People are identified by surname, and surnames repeat heavily
The provider roster underneath this world reuses surnames a lot — 5 Whitcombes, 6 Delacroix, 6 Lindqvists, 5 Santoros, 5 Templetons. Several tasks name their subjects by bare surname. Where a task also carries the person's NPI (the unique 10-digit federal provider number), that's fine. Where it doesn't, the task can become ambiguous or, in T7's case, unsolvable as instructed.

The pre-submission checklist item about surnames only covers the 11 named staff characters, which are clean. It never looks at the 249-person provider population.

Touches T1, T6, T7, T8, T9 · document A01

Several correct answers stop at the halfway point
Three tasks discover a serious compliance failure and then stop at the forward-looking half of the response. In real credentialing work, discovering that someone practiced without a valid license — or with no credentialing file at all — triggers a backward look too: what did they render, what got billed, and what has to be refunded. Under 42 CFR 401.305 an overpayment must be reported and returned within 60 days of identification, with a six-year lookback.

This isn't necessarily wrong — you may have deliberately scoped these tasks to triage only. But if the answer key says "the correct answer is X" and a credentialing professional would say the correct answer is X plus a look-back, that's a gap worth closing on purpose rather than by omission.

