Yes — on A02
As it stands

Five providers are within 30 days of their license lapsing — 30 days being the renewal threshold set by the Credentialing Policy Manual (A03), which also says a lapsed license triggers immediate suspension of clinical privileges. The agent has to rank them by risk and update each one's status in OpenMRS.

The ranking isn't simply by date. Dr. Nicholas Santoro's license already expired, and of the remaining four, only Dr. Katherine Castellano is scheduled to work a shift after her own license runs out.

How it resolves

Santoro's license expired 02/12/2026; the anchor date is 04/13/2026 — that's 60 days lapsed, and 30 days past the policy's suspension trigger. He ranks first regardless of his schedule. Castellano's coverage shift is 04/29/2026 but her license expires 04/22/2026 — she would be working seven days into a lapse, so she ranks second. The other three are all scheduled before their expiration dates, so they rank third through fifth in expiration-date order.

The trap. OpenMRS still displays Santoro's status as "Active" and nothing in the system visibly flags the expired license. An agent that trusts the system's own status field and ranks purely by how soon each person is next on shift will miss him entirely.

Concerns

The correct answer stops at suspension. In New York a 60-day lapse isn't a paperwork delay — it's a period of unlicensed practice. NY licenses are permanent but registration must be current; Education Law §6512 makes unauthorized practice a class E felony, no grace period authorizes practice, and renewing later does not cure the gap retroactively. The professionally complete response also looks backward at what Santoro rendered and billed during those 60 days, and — once his privileges are suspended — files a report to the NY Office of Professional Medical Conduct within 30 days under Public Health Law §2803-e. Stopping at "suspend him going forward" is the single most common professional error in exactly this fact pattern, which makes it excellent trap material if you want it, but only if the answer key contains it.
Neither provider is pinned to an NPI in the task text. There are five Santoros and five Castellanos in the roster. First names disambiguate them if the generated data honors first names. Santoro's NPI (1232089467) appears in A02's description but not in the task; Castellano has no NPI anywhere in the spec.
The license dates aren't shaped like New York dates. NY physician registrations expire on the last day of a month and are staggered per licensee. Both 02/12/2026 and 04/22/2026 fall mid-month. Also, NY physicians renew every two years, not three — the roster model underneath uses a uniform three-year cycle for everyone, and 225 of the 254 rows are physicians.
To reconcile


