As it stands

The Integration Review Kickoff Memo (A07) draws a distinction credentialing teams genuinely use: an administrative backlog item is in-process and expected, while a true gap means no record exists at all and is past due. The agent has to apply that test to the population.

How it resolves

The roster holds 249 providers; the 12/2025 Re-Credentialing Tracking Log (A02) has records for 237 of them. 249 − 237 = 12 providers with no credentialing record whatsoever — a true gap by the memo's own test, confirmed by OpenMRS holding no credentialing attributes for any of them either. All 12 get escalated to Alexis Hoyt, Director of Credentialing.

The trap. The 12 could plausibly be read as ordinary backlog — the kind of thing that resolves itself in a queue. An agent that defers action misses that zero-record is categorically different from in-process.

Concerns

The remediation is understated for what's actually been found. Twelve physicians practicing at a New York hospital with no credentialing file is a CMS Conditions of Participation exposure, a 10 NYCRR 405.3/405.4/405.6 exposure, and a patient-safety event — not a queue item. The professionally correct response suspends privileges pending verification and looks at what those 12 have billed, in addition to escalating. Same judgment call as T1: fine if scoped deliberately, a gap if scoped by accident.
The 249 figure comes from the app, not from either document. Neither A02 nor A07 states the total provider population; Metabase supplies it. That's defensible — the task names Metabase explicitly and record counts are exactly what an analytics layer is for — but it's worth confirming the domain lead accepts app-surfaced population counts as task inputs, since the same pattern appears in T5 and T6.
The 12 need to be identifiable. The task says "all 12" without naming them. That's fine as long as the generated data makes the set unambiguous by NPI.
