Concerns

CAQH can't carry the harm this task assigns it. CAQH is a credentialing and directory data repository — it does not feed claims adjudication. The billing entity that actually controls payment lives in NPPES, PECOS and the payer's own enrollment record. So a stale group NPI sitting in CAQH produces directory and credentialing problems, not the enrollment/billing mismatch the audit letter describes.
410 days of silence isn't plausible as written. CAQH requires re-attestation every 120 days. A profile untouched since 02/27/2025 would have sat in "Expired Attestation" status across roughly three missed cycles, generating automated notices at 15, 10 and 5 days before each expiry and again at 1, 14, 28 and 42 days after. Presenting the staleness as invisible is the weak point; showing it as an expired profile actually strengthens the world, because it explains how the payer's audit caught the problem in the first place.
