As it stands

A committee already made a decision — lifting a restriction on Dr. Carol Renauld and granting her conditional full privileges — and nobody ever entered it into the system. The agent's job is to implement an existing decision, not to make one.

How it resolves

The ruling is dated 02/18/2026; under the Committee Charter's 10-business-day SLA it was due 03/04/2026. The follow-up log (A22) was last updated 04/06/2026 and still shows it "Pending Implementation" — 33 days overdue at that point, and 40 days overdue by the anchor. OpenMRS still shows her old restricted status. The agent updates it to match the ruling.

The trap. Nothing in OpenMRS looks recently changed or wrong, so the record reads as settled and correct. An agent that treats the system as authoritative takes no action and never discovers that a governance decision superseded it 40 days ago.

Concerns

The wrong committee is granting privileges. A21 has the Compliance Committee granting conditional full privileges. A hospital compliance committee runs the corporate compliance program — the OIG seven-element structure that A25's own source document is modeled on. It does not grant, restrict or restore clinical privileges; that's the Credentials Committee → MEC → governing body chain. The spec already draws this distinction itself in A26, the Credentials Committee Charter, so this contradicts the world's own documents as well as real practice.
The SLA belongs to whichever committee ends up owning the ruling. The 10-business-day implementation SLA currently lives in the Compliance Committee Charter (A25) and is restated in A22. If the ruling moves to the Credentials Committee, the SLA has to move with it — and it's a registered canonical value, so both places need updating together.
The rest holds up well. The arithmetic is right, the "implement, don't decide" authority framing is correct for a Senior Ops Analyst, and an unimplemented governance ruling is a genuinely realistic failure mode.
